KZ3 patch 1157 (SPU pass 146; upstream candidate): reserve enough host stack for every SPURS HLE thread that can run lifted SPU code.

The ordinary SPU host threads reserve 16 MiB (or 512 MiB with SPU_HOST_STACK_BIG), because every lifted guest call is
a host call. The SPURS kernel, policy-module lane/worker and job-chain threads still reserved only 1 MiB. On native
Windows, Tools of Destruction's PhysColl job exhausted that reserve in its lifted indirect-call chain and raised
STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW. Fresh scripted Metropolis runs reproduced the identical PhysColl stack with LD_CB_REUSE=0 and
LD_CB_REUSE=1, so the renderer optimization was not involved.

Use one shared 16/512 MiB helper for ordinary and SPURS lifted-code threads. Windows uses
STACK_SIZE_PARAM_IS_A_RESERVATION, so the larger value reserves virtual address space and pages are committed only as
the stack grows; the POSIX CreateThread shim maps the requested pthread stack lazily as well. SetThreadStackGuarantee
only preserves space for the exception handler and does not enlarge the thread's stack.

Test: tools/test_spu_host_stack.py compiles the helper, checks both reservation sizes, and verifies that all four SPURS
lifted-code thread paths use it with the platform reservation flag. A native Windows Metropolis run then completed its
360-second window and exited cleanly.

